#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Bars fetched AFTER the imported sample ends. The only genuinely unseen data available.

WHY THIS IS THE TEST THAT MATTERS
----------------------------------
Every result in `tools/orb_commodity.py` was produced on data the strategy was DEVELOPED on
by the sibling project. The in-sample/out-of-sample split there splits that same sample in
half, which controls for nothing that a developer already saw. The rotation null controls for
luck, not for hindsight.

These bars were fetched on 2026-08-07 from the IBKR history feed and cover the period after
each symbol's imported sample ends. Nobody fitted anything to them.

WHAT IT CAN AND CANNOT SETTLE
------------------------------
Roughly 17 sessions per symbol. At the observed per-session standard deviation this yields a
standard error on the mean of about the same size as the claimed edge, so t will land near 1
even if the edge is entirely real. Stated in advance so the result is not over-read:

    this sample CAN detect a strategy that has stopped working outright.
    it CANNOT confirm one that still works. Do not read a positive as confirmation.

WHY ONLY THREE SYMBOLS
----------------------
MGC, silver and copper. MCL and NG are excluded, and the reason is recorded here rather than
buried: under measured costs plus the depth penalty (`tools/measured_costs.py`) MCL is
NEGATIVE (-$2,714, PF 0.91) and NG contributes PF 1.10 on a spread figure that is a
lower bound because the micro could not be quoted. Neither can change the conclusion in
either direction, so neither justifies the fetch.

That IS a selection, and it was made on in-sample performance. It is disclosed rather than
defended: the three symbols tested are the three carrying the claimed edge, which is the
right place to point an out-of-sample test, but a reader should know the other two were
dropped before the test rather than after it.

CONTRACT AND STITCHING
----------------------
Each symbol is a SINGLE expiry, not a stitched series. That is correct here and better than
stitching would be: the ORB forms its opening range, its stop and its exit entirely inside
one session, so no calculation crosses a session boundary and roll adjustment is irrelevant.
An unstitched front contract is the actual tradeable instrument.

  MGC  Dec-2026, COMEX id 751494403  (34,202 lots vs 22 in the Aug expiry: Dec is the front)
  SI   Sep-2026, COMEX id 738529133  (full-size vs micro not established; see measured_costs)
  HG   Sep-2026, COMEX id 559306209  (MHG, confirmed micro copper)

The imported sample priced SI and HG from full-size series while trading micro specs. HG here
is the actual micro. The two quote the same units, so the series are interchangeable for an
intraday range strategy, but it is a difference and it is noted.

BAR WINDOW
----------
`outside_rth=false` returns 13:30-20:30 UTC, fifteen 30-minute bars, 09:30-17:00 ET. That is
exactly the window the imported sample covers (minutes-since-open 0 to 420, 15 bars per
session), so the two are directly comparable without resampling or trimming.

TRANSCRIPTION
-------------
The arrays below are copied verbatim from the feed response. `build` asserts every array is
the same length and equals sessions x 15, so a dropped or duplicated value fails loudly
rather than silently shifting a session.
"""

